# ==========================================================================
#  O46  Defined classes: the reasoner decides membership
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    Author is defined rather than declared: equivalent to a Person who
#    wrote some Work, so that the reasoner types every such person an
#    Author without being told, and the role from module 02 has its formal
#    definition.
#
#  WHY
#    A subclass axiom gives a necessary condition. An equivalence gives
#    necessary and sufficient ones: Author is exactly the set of Persons
#    with some bs:wrote value in Work. Now membership is decided by the
#    reasoner. Rhona Blackwood is a Person and wrote a Work, so she is an
#    Author, and HermiT and the RL closure both say so from the data alone.
#    This is the construct gist's sixth rule points at: to infer instances
#    into a class, define it with someValuesFrom, hasValue or a minimum
#    cardinality.
#
#    It is also the formalisation of o08. Author was a role, anti-rigid and
#    dependent on Work; the definition says the dependence exactly, and the
#    anti-rigidity follows: a person with no works is not classified, and
#    stops being classified when the works go. Rigidity is not in the
#    axiom, but the axiom respects it.
#
#    The habit that follows is to define roles and phases with equivalences
#    and leave kinds as primitive classes. A Person cannot be defined by a
#    condition the reasoner could check; an Author can. gist's Task is
#    Event and hasGoal some Intention; its HistoricalEvent is Event and
#    actualEndDateTime some dateTime; its Person is primitive. The reasoner
#    classifies the defined ones and leaves the primitive ones to the data,
#    which is the division of labour a backbone taxonomy wants.
#
#    Two other ways to define a class are in the file for completeness. An
#    enumeration, owl:oneOf, defines bs:Country as exactly Scotland,
#    England and Wales: a closed set, the one place OWL lets you close the
#    world, and right for a list that will not change. A complement,
#    owl:complementOf, defines bs:NonFiction as a Work that is not Fiction,
#    and under the open world no individual is ever proved into it, because
#    nothing can be proved not to be fiction; gist's second rule says a
#    complement is for inferring out of a class, not into one, and the
#    measured line bears it out.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       bs:Author owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( bs:Person
#                                        [ owl:onProperty bs:wrote ; owl:someValuesFrom bs:Work ] ) ]
#
#       set reading:  A = P intersect { x : some y, wrote(x, y), y in W }
#
#       bt:author-rhona-blackwood a bs:Person ; bs:wrote bt:book-x .    bt:book-x a bs:Work .
#         =>  bt:author-rhona-blackwood a bs:Author            (HermiT; and the RL closure)
#
#       define roles and phases; leave kinds primitive
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - An equivalence to an intersection with someValuesFrom lets the
#      reasoner decide membership. Subclass gives a necessary condition;
#      equivalence gives both.
#    - Roles and phases are what to define. Kinds are primitive, because
#      no condition a reasoner can check makes something a person.
#    - The definition of Author says the dependence from module 02
#      exactly, and respects the anti-rigidity without stating it.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F07-owl%2Fo46-defined-classes-the-reasoner-decides-membership.ttl
#  SOURCES   OWL 2 Primer 5.1 Complex Classes
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/#Complex_Classes
#            gist Style Guide: OWL restrictions, the eleven rules
#            https://github.com/semanticarts/gist/blob/develop/docs/gistStyleGuide.md#owl-restrictions
#  MEASURED  parses, 48 triples; 6 classes, 1 object property, 3 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; HermiT: consistent, 6 inferred; RL closure +6; 'Who is an Author, after the ' 1 row
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bs:Person  a owl:Class .
bs:Work    a owl:Class .
bs:wrote   a owl:ObjectProperty .

bs:Author
    a                    owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel       "Author"@en ;
    skos:definition      "A person who wrote at least one work."@en ;
    owl:equivalentClass  [ a owl:Class ;
                           owl:intersectionOf ( bs:Person
                                                [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty bs:wrote ; owl:someValuesFrom bs:Work ] ) ] .

bt:author-rhona-blackwood  a bs:Person ; bs:wrote bt:book-the-loom .
bt:book-the-loom           a bs:Work .
bt:person-reader           a bs:Person .    # wrote nothing recorded: not classified

# An enumeration: a closed set, by definition.
bs:Country  a owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ; owl:oneOf ( bt:place-scotland bt:place-england bt:place-wales ) ] .
bt:place-scotland a owl:NamedIndividual . bt:place-england a owl:NamedIndividual . bt:place-wales a owl:NamedIndividual .

# A complement: a Work that is not Fiction. Nothing is ever proved into it.
bs:Fiction     a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bs:Work .
bs:NonFiction  a owl:Class ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( bs:Work [ a owl:Class ; owl:complementOf bs:Fiction ] ) ] .
# bt:book-the-loom is a Work; fiction or not, the file does not say, so NonFiction gets no member.


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Who is an Author, after the closure  (on the reasoned)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  SELECT ?who WHERE { ?who a bs:Author }
